/**

@page applications_exahype2_shallow_water ExaHyPE-Shallow

\tableofcontents

The shallow water equations (Saint-Venant) are a common (d−1) approximation to the d–dimensional Navier-Stokes equations to model incompressible, free surface flows:

\f{eqnarray*}{
\frac{\partial}{\partial t} \begin{pmatrix}
                h\\hu\\hv
               \end{pmatrix}
               + \frac{\partial}{\partial x}
               \begin{pmatrix}
                hu\\hu^2 + \frac{1}{2} \cdot gh^2\\huv
               \end{pmatrix}
               + \frac{\partial}{\partial y}
               \begin{pmatrix}
                hv\\huv\\hv^2 + \frac{1}{2} \cdot gh^2
               \end{pmatrix}
               + \begin{pmatrix}
                0\\ghb_x\\ghb_y
               \end{pmatrix} =
               \begin{pmatrix}
                0\\0\\0
               \end{pmatrix}
\f}

With @f$ b(x, y) @f$ being the bathymetry measured from a reference @f$ D @f$
(normal sea level, negative below, positive above), @f$ h(x, y, t) @f$ being the total positive water depth
and @f$ g @f$ the earth's gravitational acceleration set to @f$ 9.81ms^{-2} @f$.
The two velocities @f$ u(x, y, t) @f$ and @f$ v(x, y, t) @f$ are the velocities in x- and y-dimension respectively.

# Eigenvalues

The eigenvalues (wave speeds) in x-dimension are given by:

\f{eqnarray*}{
\lambda_{x_1} = u, \lambda_{x_2,_3} = u \pm \sqrt{gh}
\f}

And in y-dimension by:

\f{eqnarray*}{
\lambda_{y_1} = v, \lambda_{y_2,_3} = v \pm \sqrt{gh}
\f}

The respective derivations of the various aspects of the shallow water equations can be found in
"Shock-Capturing Methods for Free-Surface Shallow Flows" by E.F. Toro (2001).

# Flux

The two fluxes in x- and y-dimension respectively are given by:

\f{eqnarray*}{
F(U) = \begin{pmatrix}
       hu\\hu^2 + \frac{1}{2} \cdot gh^2\\huv
       \end{pmatrix}
\f}

\f{eqnarray*}{
G(U) = \begin{pmatrix}
       hv\\huv\\hv^2 + \frac{1}{2} \cdot gh^2
       \end{pmatrix}
\f}

# Bed Slope Term

The bed slope term

\f{eqnarray*}{
\begin{pmatrix}
  0\\ghb_x\\ghb_y
\end{pmatrix}
\f}

is implemented as a non-conservative product.

# How to Build and Run

For a general overview and all options on how to retrieve and configure the project, refer to @ref page_installation_home.
We require the NetCDF library.

## Using CMake

    cd myPeanoDirectory
    pip3 install -e .
    mkdir build && cd build
    CC=gcc CXX=g++ cmake .. -DENABLE_EXAHYPE=ON -DENABLE_LOADBALANCING=ON -DENABLE_BLOCKSTRUCTURED=ON -DWITH_MULTITHREADING=omp -DWITH_NETCDF=ON
    make -j && make test
    cd ../applications/exahype2/shallow-water/scenario/
    python3 scenario.py

## Using Automake

    cd myPeanoDirectory
    pip3 install -e .
    export NETCDF_INCLUDE_DIR=$(pkg-config --cflags-only-I netcdf)
    export NETCDF_LIBRARY_DIR=$(pkg-config --libs-only-L --libs-only-l netcdf)
    libtoolize; aclocal; autoconf; autoheader; cp src/config.h.in .; automake --add-missing
    CC=gcc CXX=g++ ./configure --enable-exahype --enable-loadbalancing --enable-blockstructured --with-multithreading=omp --with-netcdf CXXFLAGS="-std=c++20 -O3 -fopenmp -Wno-attributes $NETCDF_INCLUDE_DIR" LDFLAGS="-fopenmp $NETCDF_LIBRARY_DIR"
    make -j && make check
    cd applications/exahype2/shallow-water/scenario/
    python3 scenario.py

Where `CC=gcc CXX=g++` can be any set of compilers.

# Visualise the Simulation Output

    python3 myPeanoDirectory/python/peano4/visualisation/render.py solutions/solution-.peano-patch-file

